Answer: Your question is not really answering the exact problem you want to solve (as you write, "you have only been given the list of Clause objects"), or I would have suggested using a [matching module](https://pypi.python.org/pypi/text-unidecode/0.0.1.2). But in the case that you do really have access to the plain text file `documents-income.csv` and it hasn't got encoding problems, and you only have clauses in one column (column 2), and you want a dictionary with keys as rows and values a set of matched `Clause`, I find it more straight-forward to do what you want:

\begin{code}
from collections import defaultdict

dict_of_clause_dicts = defaultdict(lambda: defaultdict(set))
with open('documents-income.csv') as csvf:
    reader = csv.reader(csvf)
    next(reader)  # get rid of heading/description
    for row in reader:
        clauses = set(row[2]).intersection(set(Clause().clause_text))
        for c in clauses:
            key = row[0]
            print key
            dict_of_clause_dicts[key][c]
\end{code}

